  { q: 'Why do ravens follow wolves?', a: 'Ravens follow wolves primarily to access the large ungulate carcasses that wolves kill. A single raven cannot tear open a fresh deer or elk carcass — the hide is too thick. By following wolves and attending their kills, ravens gain access to meat they could not otherwise reach. This relationship is widespread across the Holarctic range overlap of ravens and wolves and is one of the most studied inter-species feeding relationships among intelligent animals.' },
  { q: 'Do wolves and ravens have a special relationship?', a: 'The raven-wolf relationship is well-documented ecologically: ravens follow wolf packs and benefit from their kills. Some researchers (Heinrich, Mech) have suggested the relationship may be partially mutualistic — ravens may alert wolves to carcasses from aerial vantage points, and the two species show unusually tolerant interactions at kills compared to raven interactions with other large predators. However, deliberate mutualism (cooperation for shared benefit) is difficult to prove, and the relationship is primarily one of commensalism (ravens benefit; wolves are largely unaffected).' },
